            <ArticleTitle>Herpes zoster of gingiva in an older woman: a rare case report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>280-283</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/ger.12246</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The aim of the article is to highlight the distinguishing features of secondary varicella gingival infection in an older women.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Herpes zoster is an acute sporadic, painful viral infection in older people caused by the reactivation of the latent varicella zoster virus. Herpes zoster affecting the gingiva without any dermal lesions is a rare pathological condition that mimics many intraoral vesiculobullous lesions. The ambiguous nature of this condition creates a diagnostic dilemma.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIALS AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 58-year-old woman presented with an acute, unilateral and persistent burning sensation and pain in the gingiva with desqaumating vesicullobulous lesion.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The women was diagnosed with secondary varicella zoster infection.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Herpes zoster of the gingiva could manifest as painful desquamative vesicular lesions, pulpal or other painful neuralgic condition in older individuals which need careful diagnosis before formulating appropiate treatment plan.</AbstractText>
